@meta
id: 19-07
title: Say no to one of them
chapter: 19
type: lesson
skills: cdo
par: 116
minutes: 7
vscode: Replace All, with two results unticked
vimrc: set nohidden
open: README.md

@brief
`OrderRepository.findByCustomer` is becoming `findForCustomer`. Two
things in the tree carry the old name and must not change, both in the
order provider: the cache key `` `findByCustomer:${customerId}` ``,
because a deploy must not throw away the warm cache, and the metric
name `'orders.findByCustomer.ms'`, because a dashboard is built on it.
Neither can be told from the real hits by a paren this time: the test's
`reveal(orderRepository).findByCustomer.mockResolvedValue` has no paren
after the name either, and that one must change.

When the pattern cannot make the distinction, you make it. Chapter 14's
`c` flag works inside `:cdo`. `:cdo s/old/new/gc | update` stops at
every match and asks; `y` changes it, `n` leaves it, and `:cdo` carries
on to the next entry either way. In a file where you answered `n` to
everything nothing changed, and `update` writes nothing, which is why
it is `update` and not `w`.

Rename the definition, the call, the mock and the assertion. Keep the
cache key and the metric.

@keys
| `:cdo s/old/new/gc \| update` | substitute on each hit, asking first |
| `y` `n` | change this one, skip this one |
| `a` | change this one and every remaining one in this file |
| `q` | stop asking in this file; `:cdo` still moves to the next |

@hints
`:vimgrep /findByCustomer/ **/*.ts` and `:copen` to see the order. Six
hits. The two strings are in the provider, and the provider's three
hits are in the middle of the list.
--
`:cdo s/findByCustomer/findForCustomer/gc | update`. Read each prompt;
it shows the line with the match highlighted.
--
In list order the answers are y, y, n, y, n, y. Then `:qa`.

@solution
    :vimgrep /findByCustomer/ **/*.ts<Enter>  :copen<Enter>
    :cdo s/findByCustomer/findForCustomer/gc | update<Enter>  y y n y n y  :qa<Enter>

This is the vim answer to "Replace All, but not that one". Each prompt
shows the line with the match highlighted, so a `n` is a decision made
with the line in front of you, not a guess from a file name. Six
prompts for six hits is quick. Sixty would be a sign that the pattern
should be doing more of the work, as it did in the previous lesson, and
the two approaches combine: narrow the pattern as far as it will go,
then confirm the remainder.

`a` says yes to this match and every later one in the same file; `q`
stops asking in this file and leaves the rest untouched. `l` changes
this one and then stops. `:cdo` moves on to the next entry after any of
them, so `q` is a per-file decision, not a way out of the whole run.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Cancelling an order refunds it through `PaymentService.sendRefund`, which
is named after the gateway's `/sendRefund` endpoint. A refund the gateway
rejects raises `REFUND_FAILED`. Refund events go out on the
`payment.sendRefund` routing key, which the ledger service consumes.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { Order } from './order.entity';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.sendRefund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }

  public async listForCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const key = `findByCustomer:${customerId}`;
    const cached = await this.cache.get<Order[]>(key);

    if (cached) {
      return cached;
    }

    const started = Date.now();
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findByCustomer({ customerId });

    this.metrics.timing('orders.findByCustomer.ms', Date.now() - started);
    await this.cache.set(key, orders);

    return orders;
  }
}

